NAME
    hier - this shell's own filesystem layout

SYNOPSIS
    man hier

DESCRIPTION
    A real Unix precedent for this exact page: 'man hier' documents a
    filesystem's layout without 'hier' being a runnable command - same here.

    /                 your home, and the whole of what this shell can see.
                      'cd' with no argument comes back here. The install
                      itself - the binaries, the repo when running from
                      source - sits ABOVE this root and is unreachable.

    /Games            your ROM library
    /Saves            battery-backed cartridge SRAM ('.srm'), a folder per
                      console: NES, SNES, N64, GB, GBC
    /Saves/Save States   'state save'/'state load' snapshots
    /Library          Mistress's own databases, written and read by the
                      program, not by hand: games.db (favourites, play
                      time, collections, edits) and records.db (who wrote
                      each save state, and the shader pack's build date).
                      Save states no longer get a .json file beside them;
                      old ones are read into records.db and left in place
    /Shaders          shader packs downloaded on request
    /Firmware         coprocessor dumps you supply
    /Cheats           your own .cht tree - see 'man cheat'
    /Logs             'dump'/'load'/screenshot/recording output
    /etc/EmuSen       every config file the emulator keeps: appsettings.json,
                      keybindings.json, gamepadbindings.json,
                      hotkeybindings.json, audio.json, graphics.json, and
                      cheats/<name>.json, and themes/<name>.axaml or
                      themes/<name>.css (either a ResourceDictionary or a
                      restricted CSS, both overriding EmuSen.LunaP's Luna*
                      keys - see EmuSen_LunaP.md). Plain JSON, meant to be read and
                      edited from this shell - comments and trailing commas
                      are tolerated. Written by EmuSen.Galaxia; these used to
                      live outside the sandbox under the OS's own per-user
                      config directory, and are copied in the first time each
                      file is read (the originals are left where they were)
    /bin              published builds only: one launcher per frontend, each
                      a two-line shim onto the real binary in /lib/EmuSen
    /lib/EmuSen       published builds only: the whole .NET application
                      directory - every shipped .dll, the apphosts,
                      deps.json/runtimeconfig.json, runtimes/. The Unix
                      '/usr/lib/<app>' shape: private libraries of one app,
                      not a shared library dir. Absent from source
    /tmp              scratch space, nothing here is ever auto-deleted.
                      '/tmp/WiseMan' is the test suite's own scratch

    /Documents        published builds only: the EmuSen Manual and the
                      per-console hardware notes under 'Man pages/', staged
                      in at publish time, or on a Mac copied in from
                      EmuSen.app. Running from source they stay in
                      the repo where they are edited, which is above this
                      root - use 'man' rather than 'cat' from source

    Every directory above is located by EmuSen.Galaxia, not by this shell -
    the sandbox forwards to it, so a core never asks a debugger where a save
    goes. See EmuSen_Galaxia.md.

    '/home' used to be five levels down, at 'EmuSen.DianaSh/DianaSh/Usr/
    Home' - a published user's save folder named after a C# project. If you
    have an install from before the move, everything the emulator wrote
    (saves, states, firmware, cheats, logs) was COPIED to '/home' on first
    run and the originals left untouched. Your ROMs were NOT copied - a
    library is too big to duplicate behind your back. They are still in
    'EmuSen.DianaSh/DianaSh/Usr/Home/{Games,Roms}'; move them into
    '/home/Games' when convenient, or just point RomDirectory at them in
    Preferences, which is where the library is configured anyway.

    A leading '/' means THIS root - your home - not the real OS filesystem
    root, and not the install directory. Every command that names a file
    ('cat', 'ls', 'find', 'awk', 'nano', redirection, 'source', 'dump',
    'load', 'cov save|load', 'label', 'cheat import|export', 'core',
    'state', 'test -f|-d') resolves through the same check and refuses
    anything above it, so no amount of '../..' walks out. Two paths are
    allowed outside the root on purpose: 'core' reads a ROM by an absolute
    path inside the library configured as RomDirectory, and 'state' with
    no path uses the frontend's own save-state path. The check is on the
    path's text and does not follow a symbolic link: a link inside the
    root that points out of it is followed by whatever opens it. Before
    2026-10-10, 'dump', 'load', 'cov', 'core', 'state' and 'test' did
    not go through the check at all.

    This shell used to be rooted at the project directory, which meant
    'ls /' listed '.git', 'EmuSen.sln' and every C# project beside your
    saves; rooting at home is what stopped that. It is a guardrail against
    accidents, not a security boundary.

WHERE THE ROOT ACTUALLY IS
    Decided once at startup by walking up from the running assembly's own
    folder (not the current directory, which a launcher or shortcut gets
    to choose and so can't be trusted), stopping at the first parent that
    holds either marker:

    'EmuSen.sln'          running from source: the project root, as
                          described above. A build output dir
                          ('bin/Release/net10.0') is three levels down, so
                          Debug and Release both land on the same root and
                          share one set of saves and logs.

    '.dianashroot'        a published build: the publish directory itself,
                          written there at publish time. No 'EmuSen.sln'
                          ships with a published app, so this is what the
                          walk finds instead - two levels up from
                          '/lib/EmuSen', where the binaries live.

    A tree published before the shell was renamed carries '.dianaosroot',
    which is found the same way.

    If neither turns up, the fallback is 'DianaShRoot/' beside the binary
    (or an existing 'DianaOSRoot/' there, when no 'DianaShRoot/' is).
    That is what a publish looks like WITHOUT the layout below - the doc
    trees stage into 'DianaShRoot/' either way, so an app dir copied out by
    hand still finds its own manual instead of coming up rootless.

    On macOS, EmuSen installs as 'EmuSen.app', which is read-only, so the
    root is '~/Library/Application Support/EmuSen' instead. It is taken
    BEFORE the walk when the program runs from inside a bundle
    ('EmuSen.app/Contents/MacOS'), and as the fallback when the walk finds
    neither marker. From source, or from a marked tree, a Mac roots where
    Linux does. '/Documents' is copied out of the bundle on every start,
    never over a file already there. See EmuSen_Galaxia.md §3.4.

    A published tree puts the binaries in 'lib/EmuSen', the launchers in
    'bin', and the shell's root at 'home' BESIDE them - so every shipped
    .dll and the running executable are now ABOVE this shell's root and
    cannot be reached from it at all.

    That was not always true. When the shell was rooted at the install
    directory, real '/' contained real '/lib', and 'rm' and 'mv' were
    suspended precisely because nothing here distinguished '/lib/EmuSen'
    from your own files. Rooting at home removed the exposure those two
    suspensions existed for; they are still suspended, which is now a
    stricter setting than the layout requires rather than a necessity.

    A published root is NOT empty. Both read-only doc trees stage into
    '/Documents' at publish time, so 'man', 'cat', 'find' and 'grep' have
    the same reference material there as they do from source. Everything
    else - the six directories under '/home' - is created empty on first
    run.

    The layout is MSBuild's doing, not this shell's: see
    EmuSen.DianaSh/Publish/DianaShPublishLayout.targets, imported by each
    publishable frontend. It only rewrites where files are copied - the
    .NET application directory is relocated whole, never taken apart, so
    nothing about how the app resolves its assemblies changes. Publish
    with -p:DianaShPublishLayout=false for a plain flat app dir.

EXAMPLES
    man hier
    ls /
    cd /home/Logs && ls
